--TEST--
`Core\Test::advance` asks whether there is a clock before it looks at what it was asked to move it by, so every duration is refused identically -- `rule:testing/determinism-declared-on-the-test`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The ordering, counted rather than read off a line. A member that validated
// its argument first would answer something *else* for at least one of these:
// the zero, the negative, and the one long enough to leave an `Instant`'s range
// are each a different answer in a member that has a clock to move, and only
// the absent clock makes them one answer here.
//
// Counting is what makes that a claim about the table rather than about a row:
// six plausible-looking refusals read the same as six identical ones on any
// single line.

use Core\Test;
use Core\Time\Duration;

array<Duration> $offsets = [
    0s,
    1ns,
    Duration::hours(-2),
    2h,
    4000d,
    Duration::days(36500),
];

int $refused = 0;
int $other = 0;
foreach ($offsets as Duration $offset) {
    try {
        Core\Test::advance($offset);
    } catch (LogicError $one) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    } catch (Throwable $two) {
        $other = $other + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused=", $refused, "/6\n";
echo "other=", $other, "\n";
--EXPECT--
refused=6/6
other=0
